  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1217 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nI am happy to say that at the moment, fog is not expected overnight \nSaturday into Sunday. Westerly surface winds that filled in across \nmuch of the forecast area behind the frontal boundary will give us a \nmuch needed break from the foggy mornings. This surface flow will \nnot last long as a cold front is expected to swing through the \nregion early Sunday morning bringing breezy northerly winds and \ncolder temperatures. Colder temperatures are expected to prevail the \nrest of the week with highs in the 40s expected everyday. Some areas \nwill even see below freezing lows. Precipitation chances are \npossible late Monday into Tuesday with an upper pattern change. The \nupper trough mentioned in the short term discussion will quickly \nexit away from the region late Saturday and we will see mostly zonal \nflow aloft to the start of next week. Models are in agreement with \nthe progression of the trough as it swings across the Desert \nSouthwest and reaches the Texas Panhandle overnight Monday into \nTuesday. However, models are not in agreement with the impact this \nupper disturbance will have on our forecast area. ECMWF has a wetter \nbias with showers and thunderstorms possible across much of the \nforecast area. GFS has a more dry bias with storms possible across \nthe far southern Texas Panhandle while the South Plains and Rolling \nPlains remain dry. As we get closer, models are expected to better \nalign.",
